     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Milton Friedman argued that shareholders should be able decide for themselves what social initiatives to donate to or to take part in, rather than having a business executive or government decide for them."
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "Freidman believed that the shareholders form the backbone of the business. The shareholders should be treated with the upmost respect. Therefore, the business should seek profit maximation. This requires the entity to find ways to generate revenue through value addition and creating more products and services while minimizing costs. Therefore, the business or government should not decide what social initiatives to donate or take part in, as the individual shareholder should handle it themselves."
    }
   ],
